💡 What is a WebSocket?

WebSocket is a protocol for a **persistent, full-duplex** (two-way) channel between client and server over one TCP connection.

Unlike classic HTTP request–response (client must ask each time),
after the WebSocket is open both sides can send messages anytime.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔄 How it works

1. Client starts with an **HTTP** handshake asking to upgrade:
   Upgrade: websocket
2. Server accepts → connection switches HTTP → WebSocket
3. Same TCP connection stays open; frames go both ways (ws:// or wss://)

────────────────────────────────────────
⚙️ Behavior contrast

Traditional HTTP (polling):
Client → "Any new messages?"
Server → "Here"
Client must ask again and again (or use long-polling)

WebSocket:
Client ↔ Server (open)
Server → "New message"   (push, no client poll)
Client → "Send message"

────────────────────────────────────────
🚀 Why WebSockets

| Reason                 | Explanation                                      |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Real-time              | Server can push instantly                        |
| Less chatter           | No repeated full HTTP request/response for each update |
| Bi-directional         | Both sides send anytime                          |
| Lower latency          | Good when delay must be small                    |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 Common use cases

| Use case           | Example                    |
| ------------------ | -------------------------- |
| Chat               | Slack, Discord-style       |
| Live dashboards    | Stocks, metrics            |
| Gaming             | Multiplayer sync           |
| Notifications      | Alerts, presence           |
| Collaborative apps | Live co-editing            |

Prefer plain HTTP/REST when: CRUD APIs, caching, idempotent GETs, no need for server push.

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Java / Spring note

`@ServerEndpoint` = Jakarta WebSocket API (works, but not the most common Boot style).

Typical Spring Boot approach:
- `spring-boot-starter-websocket`
- `WebSocketConfigurer` / STOMP (`@MessageMapping`) for messaging apps

Browser client:

const socket = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:8080/chat");
socket.onmessage = (e) => console.log(e.data);
socket.send("Hello Server!");

(See also file: `web sockets` for Spring setup detail.)

────────────────────────────────────────
🧾 Summary comparison

| Feature        | WebSocket                         | HTTP                              |
| -------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Model          | Full-duplex messaging             | Request → response                |
| Who starts     | Either side after connect         | Client initiates each request     |
| Connection     | Persistent (after upgrade)        | Often short-lived per request*    |
| Server push    | Natural                           | Needs polling / SSE / long-poll   |
| Overhead       | Low per message after handshake   | Headers each request              |
| Best for       | Real-time                         | REST APIs, resources, CRUD        |

\*HTTP/1.1 keep-alive / HTTP/2 can reuse TCP connections, but the **interaction model** is still request–response,
not a continuous two-way message channel like WebSocket.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Interview one-liner

HTTP is request–response; the client must ask for data.
WebSocket upgrades from HTTP to a persistent full-duplex channel so server and client can push messages anytime — ideal for chat, live updates, and notifications.
